Ray Dillinger wrote:
 > zahlman@gmail.com wrote:
 >> Ray Dillinger(?) wrote:

[...]

 >> I'm not sure what the intent of posting is, then.
 >> Is this a call for more developers? Just a string
 >> of YANI? Something else?

 > Something else.  This is a request for reactions,
 > to see whether there is/would be community support
 > for a successor to nethack "the way I'd make it."

A better approach to answering that queston is the
way Derek Ray did it. Just write it, purely for your
own pleasure, and see if you attract an audience who
enjoy playing it. You just cannot guess in advance
how any game is going to succeed, and no one can
guarantee you in advance that they are going to find
your game fun and adopt it as their "usual"
roguelike.

 > I have a roguelike engine mostly written already.
 > It's advanced enough in design to easily support a
 > nethack successor (or something even more complex)
 > and modular enough that you could make nearly
 > arbitrary patches that *NEVER* interfere with each
 > other.

 > I am inordinately pleased with it.

 > I'll be making a game on top of it, of course.

 > My question here was more about whether it's worth
 > my time and effort to make that game explicitly a
 > nethack successor...

 > Alternatively, I could make a completely different
 > game and let the nethack community continue to
 > wait for the next version from the devteam built
 > on the devteam's extant codebase.

I think a third approach would be of more immediate
use to the roguelike community: teach others how to
build such a roguelike engine at the philosophy,
system architecture, design, and coding level, or
alternately publish yours as GNU-licenced open
source.

Building maintainable code is surely an unknown art
in the roguelike community if NetHack is used as
s_the_ example.

 >>> * (Unicorn horns)

 >> I don't see why they're a problem as is.

 > They're a problem because they obviate all other
 > healing choices.

No they don't. There's a reason almost everyone
ascending does so with a sack full of potions of
full healing and of cram rations: unicorn horns
don't heal hit-points, and they don't heal hunger.

 > They have become "The" answer for healing, when
 > they ought to be "An" answer.

Which is the case currently. They heal stats _other_
than hit-points, they heal lycanthropy, toxic food
issues, other forms of illness, but that's not every
kind of healing in the game at all.

[Do they heal ongoing stoning? I don't know.]

If you nerf unicorn horns, suddenly you force onto
players the need for carrying sack-fulls of junk to
handle the edge cases of healing that _might_ be
needed, on the ascension run. The most important
function of unicorn horns isn't to *heal* the PC,
it's to *unburden* the PC. They currently function
exactly as apparently intended.

